# 3 Inferring a closed compiler's decisions

hls-est is not derived from a specification of Vitis HLS, because there is none at the level it needs:
the documentation [@amd2025ug1399] states what pragmas mean, not when the tool pipelines a loop it was not asked to
pipeline, how many copies of a memory it builds, or which arrays it deletes. The decision procedure has to
be inferred from the outside. This chapter describes how: what is observable (3.1), the ground truth that
every claim is measured against (3.2), the experiment loop that turns an unexplained difference into a
rule (3.3), the per-array comparison (3.4), the criterion for adopting a modelled rule (3.5), the review
cycle (3.6), and what makes the results reproducible (3.7). The method follows the micro-benchmark
tradition of measuring closed processors [@abel2019uops] and is a manual form of model learning
[@vaandrager2017model]. Section 3.8 shows the effect of the loop
over the project's rounds, and 3.9 the threats to validity. Chapter 4 states the rules the method
produced.

## 3.1 What can be observed

Vitis HLS 2025.1 is a chain of four stages with different visibility (Table 3.1). The front end is an
open-source clang/LLVM 7 fork (`hls-llvm-project` [@xilinx_hlsllvm]) and its output, `db/a.g.ld.5.gdce.ll`, is kept in
every run's `db/` directory. The closed "reflow" LLVM passes apply the pragmas, infer partitions, inline,
remove dead code and narrow types; they are visible only through the bitcode they leave after each
stage (`db/a.o.1.ll`, `a.o.2.ll`, `a.o.3.ll`, disassembled by the harness) and the `[HLS 214-*]`
messages of the compile log. The scheduler and binder are closed and not LLVM-based; their decisions
appear in the loop and memory tables of the reports, in `[HLS 200-*]` messages, in the verbose schedule
and bind reports, and in the RTL. The RAMB18 count of a memory is computed by an open-source function
(`MemoryQuerier::queryBRAM` in `CoreQuerier.cpp`), which can be read but not called. The RAMB18
primitive itself is documented in UG473 [@amd_ug473].

**Table 3.1.** Stages of Vitis HLS and where each is observed.

{{table_stages}}

Two rules follow from this. First, an observation is located at the earliest stage where it is visible:
an array missing from the memory table is traced back through `a.o.3`, `a.o.2`, `a.o.1` to the stage
where its alloca disappears, and a rule is stated at that stage (for example, dead arrays vanish at
`a.o.1`, element widths change at `a.o.2`). Second, the open source is a source of hypotheses, never of
truth: where the open formula and a measurement disagree, the measurement wins (Section 3.3, step 7).

## 3.2 The ground truth

**Configuration.** Every Vitis number in this thesis comes from one configuration (Table 3.2), the one
of the reference script the project started from. The setting that matters most is
`syn.array_partition.throughput_driven=off`. Its 2025.1 default (`auto`) partitions an array whose reads per
iteration of a pipelined loop exceed its two ports (`[HLS 214-270] ... due to pipeline pragma`); with it, polybench atax costs
{{atax_tdauto}} BRAM_18K, with `off` {{atax_tdoff}} (VITIS_NOTES N2). The `auto` behaviour is a
port-pressure heuristic layered on top of every other decision, so the ground truth disables it and
models the decisions underneath; a second result set under `auto` is kept for information only.

**Table 3.2.** Ground-truth configuration, as recorded per design in the result file.

{{table_config}}

**Harness.** `run_vitis.py` synthesises every design of the catalogue (`v++ -c --mode hls`, run on the
Windows install from WSL) and parses each run into `results/vitis.json`: totals from `csynth.xml`, the
memory, instance, loop and FIFO tables of every module's report, and the transformation messages of the
compile log. The current file holds {{gt_designs}} designs: {{gt_ok}} with a result, {{gt_failed}}
that Vitis rejects and {{gt_timeout}} that exceed the time limit, with {{gt_mem_rows}} memory rows and
{{gt_loop_rows}} loop rows in the designs with a result. Results are cached by a hash of every source and
header, the top, part, clock, tool version and configuration lines, so a design is synthesised again
only when one of them changes; experiments never write into this cache. Two runners must never share a
work root: doing so once corrupted results, and the runner now refuses it.

**Catalogue.** The designs are those of the HLSFactory catalogue (HLSFactory v1.1.0 sources plus the
pragma variants of polybench), {{cat_designs}} designs in {{cat_datasets}} datasets in the newest
comparison, of which {{cat_compared}} have both a Vitis result and an hls-est estimate. A smaller
"harness subset" of six datasets ({{subset_names}}) served as the development set before the full
catalogue was available and is still reported. From the first full-catalogue run on, the whole catalogue
was the development set: rounds started from its mismatches, so the catalogue is not a held-out test set
(Section 3.9).

## 3.3 The experiment loop

A difference between hls-est and Vitis on a corpus design is the starting point. The loop below turns
it into a rule, or into a recorded open question.

1. **Localise.** Compare per array (Section 3.4) and pick the largest per-array difference of the
   current priority. Find the first Vitis stage where the array differs from what hls-est assumes
   (the IR stages, then the reports), and the first hls-est pass where it diverges (with
   `--bram-debug-json`, Section 3.7).
2. **Hypothesise.** State the candidate rule in one sentence that can be falsified by a kernel.
3. **Minimal kernel.** Write the smallest kernel that isolates the behaviour, and a control that differs
   in exactly one respect. Kernels run through `vitis_adhoc.py` under the ground-truth configuration;
   their results go to a separate cache, never into the ground truth.
4. **Sweep one parameter.** Generate kernel families that vary one parameter across the suspected
   breakpoints: total bits around 1024, depths across the band edges, trip counts around 64, reads per
   cycle around 2 and 17, divide-by-constant values, nesting shapes. For example, the BRAM formula rests on
   {{n_aspect}} aspect-ratio and formula-reconciliation kernels (`aspect_*`, `fr_*`), the LUTRAM
   threshold on {{n_lutram}} `lutram_*` kernels and the FIFO rule on {{n_fifo}} FIFO kernels
   (`fifo_*`, `rvf_*`).
5. **Pin directives.** Where several decisions interact, fix all but one with directives
   (`bind_storage`, `pipeline` / `pipeline off`, `unroll`, `array_partition`, `allocation`) so that the
   remaining one is measured alone.
6. **Read every observation point.** The memory, loop, instance and FIFO tables; the message classes
   the harness extracts (`[HLS 214-376]` automatic pipelining, `[HLS 214-291]` implied unroll,
   `[HLS 214-449]` / `[HLS 214-270]` / `[HLS 214-421]` automatic partitions, `[HLS 200-2167]` memory
   copies, `[HLS 200-885]` port-limited II, `[HLS 214-178]` automatic inlining, and others); the IR
   stages; when needed, the verbose schedule and bind reports, the complexity dumps of the inliner and
   the RTL.
7. **Check against the open source.** Where the open-source front end or resource formula covers the
   behaviour, transcribe it and compare it with every measured row. For the RAMB18 formula, the
   transcription was checked on {{fr_date}} against the ground truth of that time, before the HLSFactory catalogue
   was merged into it. It reproduced {{fr_open}} of its {{fr_rows}} memory rows
   and {{fr_exp_open}} of {{fr_exp_rows}} experiment rows when fed the port types the binder chose; the
   measured model reproduced {{fr_n6}} and {{fr_exp_n6}}. The disagreements were each decided by new kernels
   (VITIS_NOTES N8). Re-run on the full current ground truth ({{frf_rows}} memory rows of every design with
   a memory table), the transcription reproduces {{frf_open}} rows and the measured model {{frf_n6}}. The
   difference is mostly a missing input, not a wrong formula: Vitis's own port vectors (from its bind
   reports) are available for only {{frf_vec_rows}} rows, and on those the transcription reproduces
   {{frf_vec_match}}. For the other rows the port mode (x36 or x18) is guessed from the storage type, and
   most misses are rows where the other port mode matches (`data/formula-reconcile-full.csv`).
8. **Record.** Write the rule into `VITIS_NOTES.md` with its kernels, their run directories and the
   evidence, marked *confirmed* only when standalone kernels reproduce it; a behaviour seen only in corpus
   designs stays *unconfirmed*. Wrong statements are corrected in place, with the correction marked,
   rather than deleted: the notes contain {{n_corrections}} marked corrections.
9. **Model, test, adopt or not.** Implement the general mechanism in hls-est (never a branch on a
   design, array or size), with lit tests built from the kernels, and run the catalogue; adoption
   follows Section 3.5.

**Scale.** Up to the thesis snapshot ({{exp_cutoff}}), the ad-hoc kernel cache holds {{exp_runs}} Vitis
runs of {{exp_names}} distinct kernels ({{exp_ok}} synthesised, {{exp_failed}} rejected by Vitis, most of
them on purpose, and {{exp_timeout}} timeouts); {{exp_cited}} of the kernel names appear verbatim in
`VITIS_NOTES.md`. Figure 3.1 shows when the runs were made. The notes hold {{n_notes}} entries
(N-numbers), {{n_open}} open-issue entries (O-numbers) and {{n_memos}} recorded decisions.

**Status of a note.** One parser reads the status from each note's heading, and every chapter counts
with it. A note is *confirmed* when its heading says so. Confirmed notes are further split by whether
the heading names standalone kernels ({{n_notes_confirmed_kernels}} notes) or only catalogue designs and
ground-truth reports ({{n_notes_confirmed_corpus}}: {{n_notes_corpus_list}}). A note is *partly
confirmed* when the heading says "partly confirmed" or "confirmed except where marked"
({{n_notes_partly}}). It is *unconfirmed* when the heading says so, or names a superseded rule, a corpus
observation, or data without a rule ({{n_notes_unconfirmed}}: {{n_notes_unconfirmed_list}}). Two notes
without a status are notes on method ({{n_notes_method}}). In total: {{n_notes_confirmed}} confirmed,
{{n_notes_partly}} partly confirmed, {{n_notes_unconfirmed}} unconfirmed, {{n_notes_method}} method.
Chapter 4 counts *rules*, not notes: one rule can draw on several notes and one note can feed several
rules, so its counts differ.

![Figure 3.1: ad-hoc Vitis kernel runs per day](figures/ch3-experiment-runs.png)

**Figure 3.1.** Ad-hoc Vitis kernel runs per day, by the date of each run's result file (a re-parsed run
carries the date of its re-parse).

**A worked example.** In the corpus, flowgnn pna's `neighbor_table_offsets` (a counting array `t[u] =
t[u] + 1` that is never read otherwise) costs BRAM in Vitis, while hls-est's dead-memory elimination
removed it. The hypothesis "an array whose loads feed only stores into itself is kept" was tested with a
family of kernels on one `int cnt[4096]`: only a fill (`w9r8p_dm_wo`, {{k_dm_wo}} BRAM_18K), a fill and
unused loads (`w9r8p_dm_rd`, {{k_dm_rd}}), a fill and a self-increment (`w9r8p_dm_self`, {{k_dm_self}}),
then a cycle through two arrays (`w9r8i_dm_cyc2`, {{k_dm_cyc2}}), a load that only decides a store
(`w9r8i_dm_ctl`, {{k_dm_ctl}}), and an accumulator that LICM keeps in a register (`w9r8i_dm_acc0`,
{{k_dm_acc0}}). Together they give the rule of Chapter 4 (FE-4): a store is deleted only once its array
has no used load, so a load-store cycle is never proven dead. The notes record this behaviour (N66). That
it comes from LLVM's dead-store and dead-global deletion is our interpretation, consistent with the IR
stages but not proven. The mechanism was added to `--dead-memory-elimination` behind a
configuration key and adopted after the catalogue run, whose change-log record reads: "{{dm_effect}}"

## 3.4 The per-array comparison

A total can be right for the wrong reasons, and early in the project several were: two errors of opposite
sign cancelled. `compare.py` therefore compares every design per array. hls-est writes, with
`--bram-debug-json`, one record per memory with its C variable name, banks, RAM kind and BRAM; the Vitis
side groups the memory rows of all modules by the variable they come from (partition banks `A_1_U`,
struct leaves `s_a`, RAM_1WNR copies) and multiplies a module's rows by its number of instances. Vitis
BRAM outside the memory tables (m_axi adapters, FIFOs) is matched by name (`<bundle>_m_axi`) or reported as
non-array BRAM, the design total minus the sum of its memory rows. That difference also holds the
further instances of modules whose memory rows a report lists once, and it is large: in the newest
comparison {{non_array_bram}} of the {{vitis_bram_total}} BRAM_18K of the compared designs. A comparison
of memory tables alone would miss it. The name join has a limited reach. In the final run only part of the BRAM-carrying array rows align by
name on both sides (Section 6.2 measures it), so per-array agreement is reported with its coverage and
never as a claim about every array.

A design counts as **exact** when its total equals Vitis's, and **in the goal band** when it is exact or
over-counts by at most {{band_over_pct}} % of the Vitis value (the band was 15 % until Peter narrowed it on
2026-10-24); an under-count is out of the band whatever its size,
because an estimator that under-counts would let a design that does not fit pass. Every number of the
results pages is generated from the run files by the harness scripts (`make_compare_csv.py`,
`make_hlsdoc.py`), never typed.

## 3.5 Adopting a rule

A mechanism that models a confirmed rule is not adopted automatically. The project's adoption rule (rule 4 of its working loop)
decides: **keep the change only if the net number of exact designs rises (or stays equal with a lower
error and per-array improvements), and every design that stops matching is explained per array by a
named open issue that it used to cancel.** Otherwise the change is reverted, or kept behind an option
that is off by default, and the dependency is recorded as an open issue. The rule protects against a
mechanism that is right per array but exposes a second, unmodelled behaviour that happened to cancel
the first. Table 3.3 lists the change-log entries whose mechanism was built but left opt-in or off by
default. For example, the stream-access timing of rule SC-5 matches all its kernels but would lose exact
designs until loop-carried memory dependences reach the scheduler (OPEN_ISSUES O1, O114).

The table shows the decision at the time of the entry: several of these mechanisms became defaults later,
once the issue they depended on was resolved (the integer folds, dead-memory elimination and m_axi adapters
of 2026-10-10 joined the default pipeline the same day, by recorded decision, after the shim that gives
them integer-backed fixed-point types became the default).

**Table 3.3.** Change-log entries whose mechanism stayed opt-in or off by default.

{{table_optin}}

Four designs act as hard gates for every change: the polybench_pragmas kernels bicg, gesummv, mvt and
syrk, which together exercise replication, the automatic partitions and sequential port sharing. In the
newest run they are {{gates}} (hls-est = Vitis). In addition, no lit test may fail and every design that
was exact must stay exact unless the rule-4 exception applies, with the exception named in the change
log.

## 3.6 The review cycle

Every substantive change is followed by a review by an AI agent session that did not write the change
and is instructed to break it. The reviewing sessions run on the same model family as the implementing
ones, so the review is independent of the implementing session, not of the model (Section 1.6). The
reviewer's instructions are to re-run the cited kernels under fresh names, write new kernels at the edges of
the stated rule, and check every claimed number against the run files. The defects it finds are fixed
in a separate commit, recorded in the change log. Of the {{n_changelog}} change-log entries,
{{n_changelog_review}} name a review in their title. The work was organised in rounds of four steps (prove
the rules with kernels, implement them, review, fix); the coordinating session kept {{n_round_reports}}
round reports, {{n_round_review}} of them reviews. Reviews regularly overturned
claims: a rule stated from corpus data alone was refuted by kernels (VITIS_NOTES N50, superseded by
N56), a "confirmed" label was withdrawn where the evidence was only read from a header (N37), and a
claimed exact result was traced to a fold that Vitis performs at another time (N52).

## 3.7 Reproducibility

- **Debug output tested not to change an estimate.** `--bram-debug` and `--bram-debug-json` (JSON
  version {{debug_json_version}}) report for every memory its status and reason, provenance,
  partitioning and its source, accesses with scope, II and stage, replication, RAM kind and the bank
  arithmetic. `check_debug_invariance.py` runs the estimation tests with and without the debug options
  and requires identical output. This is a test over those inputs, not a proof. The most recent
change-log record of it reads {{invariance}}. The one tool error is an old input,
`chstone_xilinx/sha.mlir`, that hls-est rejects at parse time; it is UNSUPPORTED in lit, and both runs
fail identically.
- **Regression net.** Every cgeist front-end test of the HLS corpus is mirrored as an hls-est lit test
  whose CHECK lines are hls-est's current output and whose comment holds the Vitis number
  ({{n_mirror}} MLIR files under `test/hls-est/Mirror/`); a CHECK may change only with a change-log entry naming the cause. The
  most recent change-log record of the suite reads {{lit}}.
- **Pinned inputs.** A catalogue run records the hls-est git hash and binary hash, the cgeist binary and
  its md5, the shim, the pipeline and the configuration hash; long runs use a frozen copy of the binary.
  The final evaluation run: hls-est {{run_git}}, shim `{{run_shim}}`, pipeline `{{run_pipeline}}`.
  Its MLIR was not compiled in that run but taken from an earlier run (`{{run_mlir_from}}`), whose cgeist
  binary has md5 {{run_cgeist}}. The run file records its Polygeist commit as "{{run_polygeist_recorded}}",
  because that binary was a private build. The change log names it as Polygeist `{{run_polygeist}}`, and
  the cgeist installed from that commit has the same md5 ({{run_cgeist_snapshot}}). The commit is
  therefore known from the change log and the md5, not from the run file. This is a gap in the run
  metadata.
- **Generated pages.** Every page under `hls-doc/` that shows numbers names its generator and input
  in its first line, and so do the chapters of this thesis.

