## 3.9 Threats to validity

- **One configuration.** Every rule is measured on one part (xc7z020, 7-series RAMB18), one clock, one
  tool version and `throughput_driven=off`. Rules that depend on operator latencies (Chapter 4, SC-2) or
  on the RAMB18 geometry change with the part; the configuration file of hls-est holds those
  constants, but their values for other parts are not measured.
- **Inference, not knowledge.** A rule is the simplest statement that fits its kernels and the corpus.
  Kernels are chosen by the experimenter, so a rule can fit them and still be wrong elsewhere; the
  reviews (3.6) and the corpus re-runs (3.8) are the mitigation, and the rules whose trigger is not known
  are marked unconfirmed rather than guessed.
- **Development set = evaluation set.** The catalogue whose mismatches started each round is the
  catalogue the results are reported on. Kernels were written to explain those mismatches, so they are not
  an independent sample either. Accuracy on unseen designs is not measured.
- **Repeatability of Vitis.** No ground-truth run was repeated on purpose; Section 6.8 reports the only,
  indirect evidence (copies of one design synthesised separately agree).
- **Corpus composition.** The catalogue is dominated by a few families (stream_hls, WIP, polybench
  variants, class labs); an exact count is therefore not a uniform sample of HLS code. Results are
  reported per dataset.
- **Designs without ground truth.** Designs that Vitis rejects or does not finish are excluded from the
  comparison; the estimator's behaviour on them is unmeasured.
- **Front-end fidelity.** hls-est reads MLIR from a different front end (Polygeist on clang 18) than
  Vitis's clang 7. Differences in inlining, loop shapes and constant folding change the input to every
  rule; they are measured separately (differential execution of the MLIR against the native build) and
  tracked as open issues, but not every front-end difference is visible in a BRAM total.
